From: Stamp-P4 maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sdio: drop the 4-byte GFP_ATOMIC kmalloc in esp_handle_isr (#42)

esp_handle_isr() allocated a 4-byte buffer with GFP_ATOMIC on every
interrupt to hold the INT_ST read and the INT_CLR write-back.  When the
allocation failed the callback returned without writing INT_CLR, which
per 0011's own analysis leaves the slave asserting DAT1: dw_mmc re-arms
the SDIO bit after every callback, so the IRQ spins in the exact window
a memory-pressure event creates.  kmalloc(4) in atomic context during
an order-7 reclaim storm is precisely the allocation most likely to
fail.

There is no need for a per-call allocation at all.  Replace it with a
static u32 next to the existing empty_isr_streak static:

  - SDIO IRQ callbacks for a function are serialized, and this driver
    owns exactly one sdio_func, so static storage cannot race.  The
    function already relies on that serialization for empty_isr_streak.

  - The buffer still has to be DMA-safe storage, not a stack local:
    register traffic is CMD52 bytewise today, but the opt-in
    es_reg_cmd53 probe routes reads through sdio_memcpy_fromio, and
    dw_mmc IDMAC descriptors must not point at stack addresses.
    A static (.bss) u32 satisfies the DMA API under both paths, so the
    debug probe keeps working without a probe-time allocation in the
    context struct.

With the allocation gone, every exit past the context check reaches
the INT_CLR write: the acknowledge-on-all-paths invariant 0011/0017/
0018/0020 established no longer has an alloc-failure escape.  The
!func, host_sleep and !context guards above it are unchanged -- a
missing context still cannot issue CMD52, and the sleep gate is a
deliberate suspend-state decision, not a failure.

---
 sdio/esp_sdio.c | 16 ++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/sdio/esp_sdio.c
+++ b/sdio/esp_sdio.c
@@ -367,8 +367,14 @@
 {
 	/* SDIO IRQ context only, one function at a time: no locking needed. */
 	static u32 empty_isr_streak;
+	/*
+	 * Read/ack scratch for this callback: static storage, DMA-safe even
+	 * when es_reg_cmd53 routes register reads through sdio_memcpy_fromio,
+	 * and never fails the way the removed 4-byte GFP_ATOMIC kmalloc did.
+	 */
+	static u32 int_status_store;
 	struct esp_sdio_context *context = NULL;
-	u32 *int_status;
+	u32 *int_status = &int_status_store;
 	u32 status_read;
 	u32 raw;
 	int raw_ret;
@@ -401,12 +407,6 @@
 	dispatch = context->adapter &&
 		   atomic_read(&context->adapter->state) >= ESP_CONTEXT_RX_READY;
 
-	int_status = kmalloc(sizeof(u32), GFP_ATOMIC);
-
-	if (!int_status) {
-		return;
-	}
-
 	/* Read interrupt status register */
 	ret = esp_read_reg(context, ESP_SLAVE_INT_ST_REG,
 			(u8 *) int_status, sizeof(*int_status), LOCK_ALREADY_ACQUIRED);
@@ -505,8 +505,6 @@
 	ret = esp_write_reg(context, ESP_SLAVE_INT_CLR_REG,
 			(u8 *) int_status, sizeof(*int_status), LOCK_ALREADY_ACQUIRED);
 	CHECK_SDIO_RW_ERROR(ret);
-
-	kfree(int_status);
 }
 
 int generate_slave_intr(void *context, u8 data)
--
2.43.0
